{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"fascist-gracious-quetzal","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# J9 — Make the DC transport actually drive a writing reconcile pass\n\n## Context / Problem\n\nStory J7 [rebar:b4e3-1de0-3188-40a1] landed the DC backend's port conformance and made it reachable\nthrough config, then drove it against a REAL Jira Data Center instance for the first time (GitHub\nActions run 30604756524). The writing path CRASHES:\n\n    ERROR: reconcile_once raised: 'JiraDataCenterTransport' object has no attribute\n    'set_entity_property'\n\n`--mode dry-run` passes; `--mode bootstrap-strict` exits 1. So epic e369's headline capability —\nJira Server/Data Center support — does not work yet, and the epic cannot honestly close without this.\n\nFiled as [rebar:a357-b747-ece9-4cf5]; this story is its implementation.\n\n## The root cause is a PORT defect, not only a missing method\n\n`set_entity_property` is called from THREE core sites:\n\n* `dispatch_one.py:321`, `binding_store.py:517`, `apply_inbound_records.py:279`\n\nimplemented in exactly ONE: `adapters/jira/acli_rest.py:266` (Cloud). And it is **not a member of the\n`TicketTransport` Protocol** (`_backend.py`), which declares only `create_issue`, `get_issue`,\n`update_issue`, `transition_issue_by_name`, `add_label`, `search_issues`.\n\nThe core therefore depends on a transport method the port never states. That is precisely why the\ndefect survived: `isinstance(x, TicketTransport)` is satisfied without it, the backend contract suite\nnever calls it, and 1636 reconciler unit tests are green. **Conformance to an incomplete port proves\nless than it appears to** — the same class of defect as J6's `add_label` bug, where the only oracle\nwas a fake mirroring the author's assumption.\n\nFixing only the DC transport would leave that generator of defects intact: the NEXT backend repeats\nit. So this story fixes both the instance and the class.\n\n## Approach\n\n1. Implement `set_entity_property` on `JiraDataCenterTransport` against DC's REST v2 entity-property\n   endpoint (`PUT /rest/api/2/issue/{key}/properties/{propertyKey}`), matching the Cloud\n   implementation's contract at `acli_rest.py:266` — read it first rather than inventing a shape.\n   `local_id` is the binding marker the reconciler plants to correlate a remote issue with a local\n   ticket, so a wrong shape silently breaks correlation rather than erroring.\n2. Add `set_entity_property` to the `TicketTransport` Protocol so the port states the requirement and\n   a backend missing it fails `isinstance` at construction rather than at runtime mid-pass.\n3. AUDIT for further instances of the same gap: grep `client.<method>` / `transport.<method>` across\n   the core reconciler modules, diff the resulting set against the Protocol's declared members, and\n   either add each missing method to the Protocol or record why it is deliberately outside it. This\n   is the part that stops the class of defect recurring.\n\n**Alternative rejected:** implement the method on DC and stop there. It fixes this crash and leaves\nthe port still lying about what the core needs, so the third backend hits the same wall.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira_datacenter/transport.py` — implement\n  `set_entity_property`.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/_backend.py` — declare it on `TicketTransport`.\n- `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/backend_support.py` — `FakeTransport` gains the method, since the\n  fake must satisfy the widened port (it is the contract suite's stand-in).\n- `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/` — a structural test for the audit: every transport attribute the\n  core reaches for is declared on the Protocol.\n- `tests/external/live_jira_dc/test_reconcile_pass.py` — already written by J7; it is the oracle and\n  must go from FAIL to PASS unmodified. **Do not weaken it to accommodate the fix.**\n\n## Testing\n\n- unit: `set_entity_property` issues the right REST call, asserted against a fake client — including\n  that the property KEY and VALUE reach the endpoint intact, since a silently wrong shape breaks\n  correlation without raising.\n- structural: the core-call-sites-vs-Protocol audit is a TEST, not a one-off grep, so a future core\n  module reaching for an undeclared transport method fails CI. Give it teeth with a synthetic case,\n  mirroring `test_jira_family_boundary_heldout.py::test_checker_flags_a_synthetic_violation`.\n- live: J7's `test_reconcile_pass.py` must pass in the `jira-dc-harness` CI job.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `JiraDataCenterTransport.set_entity_property` is implemented and unit-tested, with the test\n      asserting the property key and value reach the REST layer intact\n- [ ] `set_entity_property` is declared on the `TicketTransport` Protocol\n- [ ] a backend whose transport LACKS it fails an `isinstance(transport, TicketTransport)` check,\n      asserted by test — the failure moves from runtime mid-pass to construction\n- [ ] a structural test asserts every transport method the core reconciler calls is declared on the\n      Protocol, and that test is proven to FIRE on a synthetic undeclared method\n- [ ] [operator-attested] a full writing reconcile pass (`bootstrap-strict`) against the J5 harness converges with no\n      unhandled exception — J7's `test_dc_reconcile_pass_raises_no_unhandled_exception` passes for a\n      WRITING mode, not only dry-run\n- [ ] [operator-attested] an immediately repeated writing pass writes nothing (idempotence) —\n      J7's `test_a_repeated_dc_reconcile_pass_writes_nothing` passes\n- [ ] [operator-attested] a DC-created ticket carries `creation_channel == 'jira'` — J7's\n      `test_a_dc_created_ticket_carries_the_shared_jira_provenance` passes\n- [ ] the three live tests above pass WITHOUT any edit to `test_reconcile_pass.py` (`git diff` shows\n      that file unchanged) — the oracle is not adjusted to fit the fix\n\n## Note on the moved criteria\n\nThe three `[operator-attested]` live criteria were J7's. They are relocated here, not dropped: J7\ncannot satisfy them (its own scope is landed and verified), and this is the story whose fix makes them\npassable. Epic e369 therefore still requires a proven live writing pass before it closes.","id":"bda8-370d-23ad-4e36","parent_id":"e369-a449-4773-48fb","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"story","title":"J9 — implement set_entity_property on DC and make the transport port state what the core requires"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785474355307779001,"uuid":"c4823df3-52ba-430f-94e8-a59211afbd46"}